  {
    "id": "g-regular-ar",
    "title": "-ar verbs",
    "summary": "regular verbs like falar, morar, trabalhar",
    "intro": [
      "Most Portuguese verbs are regular: once you know the pattern, you know them all. The biggest group ends in -ar.",
      "Take off -ar and add the endings: -o, -as, -a, -amos, -am. Here is falar (to speak):"
    ],
    "table": [
      { "person": "eu", "form": "falo" },
      { "person": "tu", "form": "falas" },
      { "person": "ele / ela / você", "form": "fala" },
      { "person": "nós", "form": "falamos" },
      { "person": "eles / elas / vocês", "form": "falam" }
    ],
    "examples": [
      { "pt": "Falo um pouco de português.", "en": "I speak a little Portuguese." },
      { "pt": "Moras em Lisboa?", "en": "Do you live in Lisbon?" },
      { "pt": "Trabalhamos de segunda a sexta.", "en": "We work Monday to Friday." },
      { "pt": "Apanho o autocarro às oito.", "en": "I catch the bus at eight." }
    ],
    "notes": [
      "The verb ending already shows who is doing it, so eu, tu and nós are often left out: Falo inglês = I speak English.",
      "Gostar (to like) always takes de: Gosto de café. With the: de + o = do, de + a = da (Gosto do Porto).",
      "Apanhar o autocarro (to catch the bus) is the Portugal way of saying it.",
      "In Portugal, falamos = we speak, and falámos (with an accent) = we spoke."
    ],
    "drills": [
